<statement>
<p>
	First, we call the previously created display_initial_game function to get a game board. We set the color choice to be the first color in color_list (this will be the default color) Next, we get a valid click from the user (using the previously created get_valid_click_function. If the user clicks on a color, then we update the color choice accordingly. Note that the user could do this repeatedly. If the user clicks on a valid empty box, then we fill the box with the color choice and we&#x27;re done!
</p>

<tabular valign="top" top="minor">
	<col width="12%"/>
	<col width="88%"/>
	<row bottom="minor">
		<cell>Inputs:</cell>
		<cell>
			<p><pf>color_list</pf> number — is a string of 6 letters representing standard colors MATLAB recognizes</p>
			<p><pf>num_attempts</pf> (double) double — gives the number of attempts the user will have, which in turn determines the height of the game-playing area, indicated with the empty boxes</p>
			<p><pf>current_attempt_num</pf> (double) double — identifies which row of boxes are valid on the game board</p>
		</cell>
	</row>
	<row bottom="minor">
		<cell>Outputs:</cell>
		<cell>
			<p><pf>there</pf> number — are no function outputs, but there is an interactive plot</p>
		</cell>
	</row>
</tabular>

</statement>
